<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Plain HTTP is allowed for this app (issue #73).

    VAlbum talks to a *self-hosted* album server: the one the person runs at
    home, reached by IP address (192.168.x.y) or by a local host name
    (nas.local, pi) over plain http://. No per-domain rule can name that
    server, because nobody knows at build time what it is called - which is
    why the permission is in the base configuration and not in a
    <domain-config>.

    Without this, Android 9+ refuses cleartext to every *native* network
    stack. That is exactly the bug that was reported: albums and thumbnails
    load, because Dart's own HTTP client is not bound by this policy, while
    the video fails with an ExoPlayer "Source error" - ExoPlayer is bound by
    it. The Dart side already speaks cleartext to the very same server, so
    nothing is opened up here that the app was not already doing.

    Deliberately not `android:usesCleartextTraffic` on <application> as well:
    two switches for one decision, and this one is the more precise of the
    two (it keeps the system trust anchors, so an https:// server is still
    validated as strictly as before).
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
